      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>Pixle is an AI platform built for photographers, event organizers, and attendees expecting personalized access to large image libraries. The product was designed to eliminate the manual sorting step that makes large event galleries slow to process and frustrating to deliver.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to ingest large batches of event media, run recognition workflows, and serve personalized galleries without blocking users waiting for results. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve AI photo management and event media delivery use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The platform had to prove that AI could create real operational leverage for media-heavy businesses while still supporting account, payment, and delivery workflows.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Batch Ingestion</h3>
              <p>Thousands of images could arrive in compressed operational windows after events.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Recognition Throughput</h3>
              <p>Facial matching had to scale without making the interface feel stalled or unpredictable.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Media Delivery</h3>
              <p>Users expected quick access to matched galleries even when total storage volume was high.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Commercial Readiness</h3>
              <p>The product needed payment and account flows around a technically complex AI pipeline.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel 7, Blade, Azure Cognitive Services, AWS S3, Redis, Firebase, Stripe, MySQL across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">User Interface</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Workflow Orchestrator</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">AI Services</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Async Jobs</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
